update public.curriculum_units as c set description = v.description from (values
('08b67728-9b7f-49ed-aad2-98550734b58d'::uuid, 'The reproductive systems bring gametes together, but fertilisation is only the beginning of development. This theme follows the formation of the zygote, early cell divisions and implantation, showing how a single fertilised cell develops into an organised embryo.'),
('1048f954-8de1-497a-ad5c-399db55015f3'::uuid, 'Reproduction can take place without gamete fusion, allowing a single parent to produce offspring. This theme introduces asexual reproduction, the ways it occurs in different organisms and the advantages and limitations of producing genetically similar offspring.'),
('211eaa37-df23-4037-a051-02a18b78b8b7'::uuid, 'Unlike asexual reproduction, sexual reproduction involves the formation and fusion of gametes. This section examines meiosis, fertilisation and the contribution of genetic material from two parents. You will explain how sexual reproduction produces variation among offspring and why this variation matters to populations.'),
('22103e5e-9292-40d0-bcdc-5c7c905e22c9'::uuid, 'Organisms must produce offspring to maintain their populations. Some reproduce without the fusion of gametes, while others involve the joining of male and female gametes. This section compares asexual and sexual reproduction, then examines human reproductive structures and the events that follow fertilisation.'),
('2b38b19d-b028-4083-b4d7-8b3c3f88da85'::uuid, 'Sexual reproduction begins with the production of male and female gametes and continues when they fuse during fertilisation. In this lesson, you will explain the roles of gametes, describe fertilisation and relate the combination of genetic material from two parents to variation in offspring.'),
('40b9d1ad-355c-4c46-997f-77a34b0b5eaa'::uuid, 'The comparison with asexual reproduction shows that sexual reproduction depends on gametes and their fusion. This theme focuses on how male and female gametes are formed and brought together, and how fertilisation combines their genetic material to begin a new organism.'),
('41ff4202-0656-4bf2-a2cc-e88a9fa216c3'::uuid, 'The male and female reproductive systems have different structures but work together in human reproduction. In this lesson, you will identify the main reproductive organs, describe their functions and explain how sperm are produced and transported and how ova are released and moved through the female reproductive tract.'),
('829fec7e-771d-4d9d-8e01-57ba5173119f'::uuid, 'Fertilisation occurs when a sperm cell fuses with an ovum to form a zygote. In this lesson, you will describe the stages of fertilisation, explain how the zygote divides by mitosis and outline how the developing embryo moves to and implants in the uterus.'),
('99af1661-b7e0-4686-9f5d-8fe4505c3250'::uuid, 'Asexual reproduction involves one parent and does not require the fusion of male and female gametes. In this lesson, you will describe common methods such as binary fission, budding and vegetative propagation, and explain why offspring produced in this way are usually genetically similar to the parent.'),
('a3160199-9bfb-4e68-9984-50f4cb313f2b'::uuid, 'Human reproduction depends on specialised male and female organs that produce gametes and support fertilisation and development. This section examines the structure and functions of the reproductive systems, including the pathways through which sperm and ova move.'),
('b5d59442-0781-4df0-929f-210e404066b7'::uuid, 'The formation and fusion of gametes require specialised reproductive organs. This theme introduces the male and female reproductive systems, linking the structure of each organ to gamete production, transport and the conditions needed for fertilisation.'),
('de8cf625-0382-4aa1-9d60-93aa078010e6'::uuid, 'Once a sperm cell reaches an ovum, fertilisation may occur and development begins. This section follows the events from fertilisation through early embryonic development and implantation. You will examine how the zygote divides and how the developing embryo becomes established in the uterus.')
) as v(id,description) where c.id=v.id and c.description is distinct from v.description;
